  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Increased capacity of Members States to make social dialogue more influential and labour relations and labour justice institutions and processes more effective</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">GLO423 is the ILO&#x2019;s compendium of global outputs aimed at strengthening Member States&#x2019; institutional and technical capacity to make social dialogue more influential in policymaking, and to make labour relations and labour justice systems more effective, inclusive, accessible and rights-based. The outputs will be developed globally, drawing on evidence, comparative analysis, international labour standards and regional inputs. They will support coherent, demand-driven and context-sensitive interventions during the 2026&#x2013;27 biennium and beyond, by ILO specialists and tripartite constituents, as global references or adapted to national, sectoral or institutional contexts.
The compendium will provide tools for policy development, institutional strengthening, capacity building, advocacy, knowledge generation, resource mobilization, and communication. It will promote consistency in ILO support while allowing flexibility across diverse labour markets, legal frameworks and social dialogue institutions.
The outputs are grouped into five areas:

1) Research and knowledge products, including analytical studies, comparative reviews, diagnostics, case studies, and evidence-based resources.
2) Policy guidance, advocacy and capacity-building tools, including guides, training materials, methodologies and technical resources for governments, employers&#x2019; and workers&#x2019; organizations, and labour institutions.
3) Flagship reports, recurrent item discussion reports, and other major publications, providing authoritative analysis, policy messages, and strategic direction.
4) A Plan of Action for resource mobilization to support progress towards SDG Target 8.8 by identifying priorities, partnerships, and funding opportunities.
5) Outreach and communication tools, including the ratification campaign, to increase visibility, promote uptake of ILO standards and guidance, and strengthen engagement with constituents and partners.</narrative>
  </description>
